==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|*squalen}} {{q|not recorded}} and {{m|enm|squelen|t=to cry, scream, squall}}, from {{der|en|non|skvala|t=to cry out}}, probably ultimately {{onomatopoeic|en|title=imitative}} with influence from {{m|en|squeal}} and {{m|en|bawl}}.

Cognate with {{cog|sv|skvala|t=to gush, pour down}}, {{cog|no|skval|t=sudden rush of water}}. The noun is probably from the verb.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈskwɔːl/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-squall.wav|Audio (UK)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔːl|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|meteorology}} A [[squall line]], multicell line, or part of a squall line.
# {{lb|en|often|nautical}} A sudden [[storm]], as found in a squall line.
# A loud [[cry]] or [[wail]].
#*{{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=34|page=163|passage=But the third Emir, now seeing himself all alone on the quarter-deck, seems to feel relieved from some curious restraint; for, tipping all sorts of knowing winks in all sorts of directions, and kicking off his shoes, he strikes into a sharp but noiseless '''squall''' of a hornpipe right over the Grand Turk’s head; and then, by a dexterous sleight, pitching his cap up into the mizentop for a shelf, he goes down rollicking so far at least as he remains visible from the deck, reversing all other processions, by bringing up the rear with music.}}

